Z||000000||Black88GT||08-02-2001||04:10 PM||||I got this coupe home, cleaned it up a bit, now I am ready to figure out what the problem is.  <P>The P.O. said that his son was "speed shifting" and then it wouldnt go into gear anymore, had to tow it home.  I am thinking its one of the cables, but they didn't appear to be broken.  Would it be worth installing new cables to see if that works?  Or do you think the tranny is shot?  Any tell tale signs?  <P>On a side note, anyone know if the 2.5 from the 1990 Pontiac 6000 is any different then the Fiero one?  Because this one has the 6000 motor in it.<P>TIA<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff (AOL IM - FieroTT)<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD - MAFOA<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://88database.homestead.com/files/AF.gif">      <BR><I>"Once you go black, you never go back..."</I><BR><I>"The Customer Can Have Any Color He Wants So Long As It's Black" </I> -Henry Ford||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||08-02-2001||04:45 PM||||I've heard the shift forks are supposed to be the weak link in the Isuzu tranny.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I'd just check and see if the tranny ends of the shift cables are doing anything at all. You might have got lucky and the kid just broke a cable or something.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||08-02-2001||05:16 PM||||I agree w/ Raydar.  It sounds like the speed shifting may have bent one or both of them.  A good way to tell once you verify its not the cables is to disconnect the cables and go through the gears using the shifting levers.  GL||||reg||1
